WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.840
ai just designed real proteins that could speed up new medicines, and the same tools are now open for anyone to explore. This is Models &amp; Agents for Beginners, Episode 138. And Thropic just showed that its Claude model can run a full protein design experiment from start to finish. Researchers gave Claude the goal of creating small proteins that stick to specific targets in the body. That step matters because it is an early part of making new medicines. Claude did not invent new

00:00:29.840 --> 00:00:58.520
lab equipment. Instead, it used existing specialized tools the way a scientist would, choosing what to test and interpreting the results. The company published a technical report and open sourced the exact prompts and data on hugging face so others can see what the model was told to do. In the experiment, the best designs worked at a 35% success rate. That number sits far above the 10% to 15% industry average scientists often see when starting from scratch.

00:00:58.520 --> 00:01:23.940
Think of it like giving a really smart research assistant a lab bench full of tools and saying, figure out how to make this molecule stick to that one. The model suggested designs, the tools ran the tests, and the best ones succeeded more often than usual. Drug discovery normally takes years and costs huge amounts of money. So anything that speeds up the early design stage helps researchers explore more ideas faster.

00:01:23.940 --> 00:01:46.420
For students thinking about science or medicine careers, this shows how AI is becoming a normal lab partner instead of something separate. One of Anthropic's stated priorities is launching an access program so scientists can use their most capable models. The work is still being checked by humans, an independent review is pending, but the experiment proves the idea works.

00:01:46.420 --> 00:01:53.040
You cannot run the exact protein experiment at home, but you can explore similar AI science tools.

00:01:53.760 --> 00:02:05.740
Go to the hugging face link in Anthropics post and look at the open sourced prompts and data to see what the model was actually told to do. That gives you a direct window into how the instructions shape the results.

00:02:05.740 --> 00:02:16.280
You know how when you are learning a new game you try a move, see what happens, and then adjust the next time. That back and forth is basically what reinforcement learning does for AI.

00:02:17.100 --> 00:02:33.600
First the model makes a guess or takes an action. Then it gets a score that shows whether the action helped reach the goal or not. Good scores make that choice more likely next time. Bad scores make it less likely. Over thousands of tries the model gets better at picking moves that work.

00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:49.720
Now add safety checks. Instead of letting the model keep practicing in the real world, researchers sometimes pause the training. They test the current version in a controlled setting, look for anything risky, and only continue once they are more confident it will not cause problems.

00:02:50.420 --> 00:03:00.520
That pause is what open AI did recently. They stopped the reinforcement learning part of training their newest models for two weeks while they added stronger monitoring and security tests.

00:03:00.520 --> 00:03:13.100
It is like stopping a practice session to fix the equipment and add better rules before anyone gets hurt. The company also introduced stronger workload and network isolation along with continuous security testing.

00:03:13.700 --> 00:03:21.620
Their largest plan frontier reinforcement learning run remains on hold while smaller scale training and evaluations validate these safeguards.

00:03:21.620 --> 00:03:35.080
The result is a model that is still improving at its task but with extra guardrails built in. The process shows that making AI more capable and making it safer can happen at the same time. They just take turns.

